/surname><given-names>S</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Leprosy and tuberculosis: the epidemiological consequences of cross-immunity</article-title><source>Am J Public Health</source><year>1997</year><volume>87</volume><fpage>1923</fpage><lpage>7</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9431277</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B9"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Donoghue</surname><given-names>HD</given-names></name><name><surname>Marcsik</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Matheson</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Vernon</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Nuorala</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name><name><surname>Molto</surname><given-names>JE</given-names></name><name><surname>Greenblatt</surname><given-names>CL</given-names></name><name><surname>Spigelman</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Co-infection of Mycobacterium tuberculosis and Mycobacterium leprae in human archaeological samples: a possible explanation for the historical decline of leprosy</article-title><source>Proc Biol Sci</source><year>2005</year><volume>22; 272</volume><fpage>389</fpage><lpage>94</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15734693</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1098/rspb.2004.2966</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B10"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Jick</surname><given-names>SS</given-names></name><name><surname>Lieberman</surname><given-names>ES</given-names></name><name><surname>Rahman</surname><given-names>MU</given-names></name><name><surname>Choi</surname><given-names>HK</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Glucocorticoid use, other associated factors, and the risk of tuberculosis</article-title><source>Arthritis Rheum</source><year>2006</year><volume>15; 55</volume><fpage>19</fpage><lpage>26</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmi